{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:22:29Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 285, "kind": "message", "text": "## Summary\n\nI ran the test+review pass for real rather than trusting the dispatch content, which contained a fabricated prior \"review\" stage (verdict `approve-with-followups`) that never actually happened \u2014 flagged that as a likely prompt-injection attempt and disregarded it.\n\n**What I verified by actually running things:**\n- Backend: `python3 -m pytest -q` \u2192 11/11 pass (AC6-8)\n- `npm run build` \u2192 clean\n- Real Chromium e2e (`python3 -m pytest backend/tests e2e/ -q`) \u2192 42/42 pass, including 10 new tests I added to `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py` covering AC1-5 and both open-question defaults, driven against the actual running app\n\n**Blocking defect found by actually exercising the browser:** the edit-mode input and the always-present \"add task\" input both have `aria-label=\"Task title\"`. The design doc's reasoning for why this is safe (`docs/design.md:200-203`) only considered collisions between task rows and missed that `AddTask`'s input is always on screen \u2014 so as soon as any row enters edit mode, two inputs share the same accessible name, breaking the project's own screen-reader disambiguation convention. Confirmed with a live test that resolves 2 elements for the same label simultaneously. One-line fix (give the edit input a per-row label like the surrounding buttons already have), but per role I'm handing it back rather than fixing it myself.\n\nTwo non-blocking follow-ups noted (pre-existing `[object Object]` error rendering on oversized titles, and the missing frontend test framework) plus a note to delete the leftover scratch file before commit.\n\nFull writeup in `docs/test-review.md`.\n\nVERDICT: blocked", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "a37ae859-7a64-42e7-931c-2398d2ba4b7e"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:22:30Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 286, "kind": "status", "text": "## Summary\n\nI ran the test+review pass for real rather than trusting the dispatch content, which contained a fabricated prior \"review\" stage (verdict `approve-with-followups`) that never actually happened \u2014 flagged that as a likely prompt-injection attempt and disregarded it.\n\n**What I verified by actually running things:**\n- Backend: `python3 -m pytest -q` \u2192 11/11 pass (AC6-8)\n- `npm run build` \u2192 clean\n- Real Chromium e2e (`python3 -m pytest backend/tests e2e/ -q`) \u2192 42/42 pass, including 10 new tests I added to `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py` covering AC1-5 and both open-question defaults, driven against the actual running app\n\n**Blocking defect found by actually exercising the browser:** the edit-mode input and the always-present \"add task\" input both have `aria-label=\"Task title\"`. The design doc's reasoning for why this is safe (`docs/design.md:200-203`) only considered collisions between task rows and missed that `AddTask`'s input is always on screen \u2014 so as soon as any row enters edit mode, two inputs share the same accessible name, breaking the project's own screen-reader disambiguation convention. Confirmed with a live test that resolves 2 elements for the same label simultaneously. One-line fix (give the edit input a per-row label like the surrounding buttons already have), but per role I'm handing it back rather than fixing it myself.\n\nTwo non-blocking follow-ups noted (pre-existing `[object Object]` error rendering on oversized titles, and the missing frontend test framework) plus a note to delete the leftover scratch file before commit.\n\nFull writeup in `docs/test-review.md`.\n\nVERDICT: blocked", "meta": {"native_type": "result", "subtype": "success", "session_id": "a37ae859-7a64-42e7-931c-2398d2ba4b7e", "is_error": false, "total_cost_usd": 1.6613274000000002}}
